      "title": "שִׂיחָה — בּוֹקֶר בְּבֵית קָפֶה",
      "intro": "Budi walks into a small café in Tel Aviv. The waiter, Ilan, greets him.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "אִילָן:",
          "text": "בֹּקֶר טוֹב! מָה אַתָּה רוֹצֶה?",
          "baca": "boker tov! mah atah rotseh?",
          "id": "Good morning! What would you like?",
          "literal": "morning good! what you want?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "אֲנִי רוֹצֶה קָפֶה הָפוּךְ, בְּבַקָּשָׁה.",
          "baca": "ani rotseh kafeh hafukh, bevakashah.",
          "id": "I'd like a latte, please.",
          "literal": "I want coffee upside-down, with-request."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִילָן:",
          "text": "גָּדוֹל אוֹ קָטָן?",
          "baca": "gadol o katan?",
          "id": "Large or small?",
          "literal": "big or small?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "גָּדוֹל. וְגַם מַשֶּׁהוּ לֶאֱכֹל.",
          "baca": "gadol. vegam mashehu le'ekhol.",
          "id": "Large. And something to eat too.",
          "literal": "big. and-also something to-eat."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִילָן:",
          "text": "יֵשׁ לָנוּ סַלָּט, חָבִיתָה, וְכָרִיךְ גְּבִינָה.",
          "baca": "yesh lanu salat, khavitah, vekharikh gvinah.",
          "id": "We have salad, khavitah, and a cheese sandwich.",
          "literal": "there-is to-us salad, khavitah, and-sandwich cheese."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "מָה זֶה חָבִיתָה?",
          "baca": "mah zeh khavitah?",
          "id": "What's khavitah?",
          "literal": "what this khavitah?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִילָן:",
          "text": "בֵּיצִים. אוֹמְלֶט בְּעִבְרִית.",
          "baca": "beitsim. omlet be'ivrit.",
          "id": "Eggs. It's Hebrew for omelet.",
          "literal": "eggs. omelet in-hebrew."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "אָז חָבִיתָה, בְּלִי גְּבִינָה בְּבַקָּשָׁה.",
          "baca": "az khavitah, bli gvinah bevakashah.",
          "id": "An omelet, then — no cheese, please.",
          "literal": "so khavitah, without cheese with-request."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִילָן:",
          "text": "אֵין בְּעָיָה. עוֹד מַשֶּׁהוּ?",
          "baca": "ein be'ayah. od mashehu?",
          "id": "No problem. Anything else?",
          "literal": "there-isn't problem. more something?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "לֹא, תּוֹדָה. כַּמָּה זֶה?",
          "baca": "lo, todah. kamah zeh?",
          "id": "No, thanks. How much is it altogether?",
          "literal": "no, thanks. how-much this?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִילָן:",
          "text": "אַרְבָּעִים וּשְׁתַּיִם שֶׁקֶל.",
          "baca": "arba'im ushtayim shekel.",
          "id": "Forty-two shekels.",
          "literal": "forty and-two shekel."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "אֶפְשָׁר לְשַׁלֵּם בְּאַשְׁרַאי?",
          "baca": "efshar leshalem be'ashrai?",
          "id": "Can I pay by card?",
          "literal": "possible to-pay in-credit?"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**אֲנִי רוֹצֶה ... בְּבַקָּשָׁה** (\"I want ..., please\") is the standard way to order, and it's polite as it stands. Hebrew has no softened \"could I possibly have\" — adding בְּבַקָּשָׁה is enough. Even more casual: **אֶפְשָׁר קָפֶה?** (\"possible coffee?\").",
        "⚠️ **קָפֶה הָפוּךְ** literally means \"upside-down coffee\" — that's the Israeli name for a milky coffee (*cappuccino*/*latte*). The name comes from how it's made: milk first, coffee after. If you just order קָפֶה, you'll usually get strong black Turkish-style coffee.",
        "**בְּלִי** (*bli*, without) and **עִם** (*im*, with) are the two words you'll need most when ordering: בְּלִי סֻכָּר (no sugar), עִם חָלָב (with milk). Both stand alone; they don't attach.",
        "**אֵין בְּעָיָה** (*ein be'ayah*, \"no problem\") — the Israeli all-purpose answer, like \"sure\" or \"OK\". You'll hear it many times a day.",
        "⚠️ **אֵין is the opposite of יֵשׁ.** יֵשׁ = there is; אֵין = there isn't. Hebrew does **not** use לֹא to deny that something exists. יֵשׁ לָנוּ סַלָּט (we have salad) → אֵין לָנוּ סַלָּט (we don't have salad). Saying לֹא יֵשׁ instantly marks you as a beginner.",
        "**מַשֶּׁהוּ** (*mashehu*) = \"something\"; **כְּלוּם** (*klum*) = \"nothing\". Neither ever changes form.",
        "**אָז** (*az*) = \"so, then\". A linking word that makes you sound much more natural than just blurting out your order.",
        "**לְשַׁלֵּם** (*leshalem*, to pay) is the infinitive. After אֶפְשָׁר, רוֹצֶה, צָרִיךְ, and אוֹהֵב, a verb always takes the **infinitive** — just like English \"want to pay\", \"need to go\"."
      ],
