          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Two things to know about eating in Israel",
          "items": [
            "**כָּשֵׁר** *(kasher)* marks food that follows Jewish dietary law. What visitors notice most: meat and dairy aren't served together, so many restaurants pick one — **בְּשָׂרִי** *(besari, meat)* or **חֲלָבִי** *(khalavi, dairy)*. A חֲלָבִי restaurant serves no meat at all.",
            "**A tip (טִיפּ) is about 10–12 percent** and often has to be paid in cash even if the meal goes on a card. The sentence: אֶפְשָׁר לְהוֹסִיף טִיפּ? *(efshar lehosif tip?)*"
          ]
        }
